WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.240
This vibration monitor uses an IRIV IO

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.779
Controller, running the Wilcoxon gateway firmware.

3
00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:08.832
It reads a Wilcoxon 883M, and

4
00:00:08.832 --> 00:00:11.663
publishes to Viking Analytics over MQTT.

5
00:00:12.883 --> 00:00:14.253
The sensor measures.

6
00:00:14.253 --> 00:00:16.856
The controller polls it and publishes.

7
00:00:16.856 --> 00:00:19.321
Viking Analytics learns the machine,

8
00:00:19.321 --> 00:00:20.897
and flags what changes.

9
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.100
The 883M sits on the motor, and

10
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.281
measures vibration on three axes, plus temperature.

11
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:30.317
The controller polls it over Modbus,

12
00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:32.504
and publishes each reading as MQTT.

13
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.508
MultiViz learns what normal looks like.

14
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:41.344
The 883M is a triaxial MEMS sensor.

15
00:00:41.344 --> 00:00:43.035
±16 g, 2 Hz to

16
00:00:43.035 --> 00:00:45.693
5 kHz, sealed to IP67.

17
00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:48.763
Its RS485 pair lands on A

18
00:00:48.763 --> 00:00:50.536
and B, with ground on G.

19
00:00:50.536 --> 00:00:52.826
The controller ends the bus, so

20
00:00:52.826 --> 00:00:55.042
the terminator switch goes on.

21
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.436
A 24 V supply powers the controller,

22
00:00:58.436 --> 00:01:00.621
and the sensor shares the same rail.

23
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.849
Install the firmware from the web installer.

24
00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:07.093
Then set the MQTT broker and the alarm

25
00:01:07.093 --> 00:01:09.751
limits in the controller's own web dashboard.

26
00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:13.391
MultiViz learns a baseline for each sensor,

27
00:01:13.391 --> 00:01:15.351
so there are no thresholds to tune.

28
00:01:15.351 --> 00:01:17.927
When the pattern shifts, it names the symptom,

29
00:01:17.927 --> 00:01:20.503
and ranks which machine needs attention first.

30
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:22.679
Now the alarm.

31
00:01:22.679 --> 00:01:25.428
Output zero follows the dashboard alarm,

32
00:01:25.428 --> 00:01:27.008
and drives a 24 V relay

33
00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:28.657
for a beacon and buzzer.

34
00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:31.331
A push button on input

35
00:01:31.331 --> 00:01:33.108
one acknowledges the alarm.

36
00:01:33.108 --> 00:01:35.149
Input zero can start a capture.

37
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.016
Vibration on the motor climbs

38
00:01:38.016 --> 00:01:39.187
past the alarm limit.

39
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.284
After the delay, the controller trips the alarm.

40
00:01:43.284 --> 00:01:45.147
Output zero closes the relay,

41
00:01:45.147 --> 00:01:46.624
and the beacon flashes.

42
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.550
The operator presses acknowledge.

43
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.504
While vibration is still high,

44
00:01:51.504 --> 00:01:52.742
the relay stays on.

45
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.194
Once it clears, the relay drops,

46
00:01:55.194 --> 00:01:56.820
and the alarm is ready again.

47
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.255
The rule runs on the controller.

48
00:02:00.255 --> 00:02:02.517
Trip on velocity or temperature, after

49
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:04.243
a delay, and latch the relay.

50
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:06.088
It drops only when the alarm is

51
00:02:06.088 --> 00:02:08.588
acknowledged, and the reading has cleared.

52
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:11.270
Sense, analyse, act.

53
00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.740
One sensor, one controller, and an

54
00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.138
alarm that someone has to answer.

